70 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 45 0 R
/E (   Your company manages a diversified shipment network serving customers across multiple regions and product categories. The Strait of Hormuz has historically provided an efficient corridor for a significant part of the company's business. During the 2026 disruption, however, the economics of that network changed quickly.)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
71 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 45 0 R
/E (  The disruption did not create one single problem. It created a chain of effects: some shipments were rerouted, some remained exposed to the disrupted corridor, some incurred higher freight and fuel costs, some attracted higher insurance costs, some missed DIFOT commitments, and some remained held in the Gulf with revenue not yet recognised. At the same time, the contracted freight revenue on the underlying business was largely fixed.)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
72 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 45 0 R
/E (   This creates the central commercial tension of the case: the company cannot assume that a higher logistics cost can immediately be recovered through price. A route can therefore be operationally feasible but commercially unattractive; a more expensive route can be strategically justified for a critical customer; and a shipment with apparently attractive revenue can still destroy margin once delay, insurance and service consequences are included.)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
